#include <cstddef>
#include <cstdint>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

#include <fuzzer/FuzzedDataProvider.h>

#include "/src/double-conversion/double-conversion/strtod.h"
#include "/src/double-conversion/double-conversion/utils.h"

extern "C" int LLVMFuzzerTestOneInput(const uint8_t *data, size_t size) {
FuzzedDataProvider provider(data, size);

// The 'exponent' argument should be an integer value that, when combined with
// the number of digits in the 'buffer', does not cause an overflow or
// underflow. The valid range for exponent + buffer.length() is between -324
// and 309, inclusive.
// We generate an exponent in a slightly larger range to test edge cases.
int exponent = provider.ConsumeIntegralInRange<int>(-400, 400);

// The Strtod function is expected to handle various string formats, including
// those with whitespace. By passing the raw string, we allow the fuzzer to
// test the internal TrimAndCut function, which handles parsing.
std::string str_to_convert = provider.ConsumeRemainingBytesAsString();

// The buffer must not be empty for the vector constructor.
if (str_to_convert.empty()) {
return 0;
}

double_conversion::Vector<const char> buffer(
reinterpret_cast<const char *>(str_to_convert.data()),
str_to_convert.length());

// Call the target function with the raw (un-sanitized) buffer to exercise
// its internal parsing and trimming logic.
double_conversion::Strtod(buffer, exponent);

return 0;
}
</fuzz-target>

<function-requirements>

<input>
project name: double-conversion
function signature: double double_conversion::Strtod(Vector<const char>, int)
</input>

<description>
The `Strtod` function converts a string representation of a number to a double-precision floating-point value. The function first preprocesses the input string by calling `TrimAndCut` to remove leading and trailing whitespace and to truncate the string to a maximum of 780 significant digits. The resulting trimmed string is then passed to `StrtodTrimmed`, which performs the core conversion logic. `StrtodTrimmed` handles a variety of cases, including numbers that can be represented exactly as doubles and numbers that require more complex conversion algorithms involving high-precision arithmetic. It also correctly handles overflow and underflow conditions, returning infinity or zero as appropriate. The `exponent` argument is an integer that specifies the power of 10 by which the number represented by the buffer should be multiplied. This allows the function to handle numbers in scientific notation. The combination of the buffer and the exponent determines the final value of the converted number. The function is designed to be highly accurate and to correctly handle edge cases, such as numbers that are very close to the limits of the double-precision floating-point format.
</description>

<requirements>

<requirement>
The `buffer` argument should be a `Vector<const char>` containing a sequence of characters that can be parsed as a number. This is because the function will pass the buffer to the `TrimAndCut` function, which will then be processed by `StrtodTrimmed`, and finally by `ReadUint64` which expects a sequence of digits.
</requirement>
<requirement>
The `exponent` argument should be an integer value that, when combined with the number of digits in the `buffer`, does not cause an overflow or underflow. The valid range for `exponent + buffer.length()` is between -324 and 309, inclusive.
</requirement>
<requirement>
The `buffer`'s length should be less than or equal to `kMaxSignificantDecimalDigits` (780). This is because the `TrimAndCut` function will truncate the buffer to this length before further processing.
</requirement>

</requirements>

</function-requirements>

